0682: \begin{definition}[Operational acceptance interface]
0683: \label{def:operational-acceptance-interface}
0684: A \emph{claim} is a manuscript assertion that can affect a mathematical,
0685: artifact, route, or submission decision.  A \emph{load-bearing claim} is a claim
0686: whose removal would change a theorem statement, a case-study conclusion, or the
0687: submission-readiness status.  An \emph{advisory output} is any AI-agent draft,
0688: score, suggested theorem, review packet, or repair proposal before a gate records
0689: it.  A \emph{deterministic gate} is a repeatable predicate, script, or checklist
0690: whose inputs and pass/fail decision are recorded independently of the prose
0691: generated by an agent.  A \emph{human promotion} is the explicit editorial act
0692: that moves a candidate into publication state, for example by creating an active
0693: \texttt{2026\_*} paper directory with \texttt{main.tex} and \texttt{PIPELINE.md}.
0694: An \emph{evidence surface} is the set of source paths, commits, commands, logs,
0695: inventories, and witness records cited for a claim.  An \emph{acceptance entry}
0696: is a manuscript or submission-ledger record that marks a candidate claim as
0697: usable in the publication-facing argument.  \emph{Accepted work} is the subset of
0698: candidate claims with such an entry.  This is a status notion only: the
0699: definition records that an editorial acceptance entry exists, but it does not by
0700: itself assert external editorial acceptance or implementation correctness.  A
0701: pipeline is \emph{record-gate-sound for acceptance} when each acceptance entry records
0702: the claim kind, the evidence surface, the evidence level, and a pass record for
0703: every gate in $\Gamma$ assigned to that claim kind, and when a route-boundary
0704: entry also records human promotion.
0705: 
0706: The deterministic dispatch function $\Gamma$ from claim kinds to gates is part
0707: of the operational interface.  Its domain is
0708: \[
0709: \{\text{formal-source},\text{ traceability},\text{ route},
0710: \text{ submission},\text{ artifact},\text{ discovery},\text{ work-selection}\}.
0711: \]
0712: It sends formal-source claims to the build and axiom-purity gates; traceability
0713: claims to inventory and marker-existence gates; route claims to promotion,
0714: overlap, and submission-state gates; submission claims to submission-pack gates;
0715: artifact claims to status and limitation-ledger gates; discovery claims to the
0716: pinned discovery gate above and, for extension claims, to the theorem-content
0717: delta gate; and work-selection claims to the scheduler gate, whose evidence
0718: surface is a critical-path score or backlog window together with the claim kind
0719: that the selected item is meant to advance.  For mixed claims, $\Gamma$ returns
0720: the union of the gate sets for the claim kinds present.
0721: 
0722: The route gates have separate operational meanings.  The \emph{promotion gate}
0723: uses the active-paper evidence surface, namely a human promotion act producing a
0724: \path{2026_*} directory with \path{main.tex} and \path{PIPELINE.md}; passing
0725: means the candidate may be daemon-visible publication work, and failing means it
0726: remains intake or source material.  The \emph{overlap gate} uses route identity,
0727: semantic-overlap ledgers, submitted-sibling paths, and program-board state;
0728: passing means advancement is route-disjoint or has a recorded resolution, and
0729: failing means advancement is blocked until closure, merge, supersession, or
0730: waiting for prior feedback.  The \emph{submission-state gate} uses submission
0731: markers, venue state, board state, and submission-pack metadata; passing means
0732: the route's publication state permits the proposed action, and failing means the
0733: action would duplicate, race, or under-specify an active or submitted route.  The
0734: \emph{theorem-content delta gate} uses theorem inventories, hard-lint output, and
0735: delta records such as \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md}; passing means that an
0736: extension claim identifies load-bearing mathematical content, and failing means
0737: that compilation, file churn, or surface edits may not be reported as substantive
0738: theorem growth.
0739: 
0740: A \emph{reviewable artifact} for this paper is either a public repository file,
0741: a supplemental file included with the submission, or an explicit
0742: historical-evidence boundary stating that the local path is used only as
0743: path-verified evidence and not as a fresh command-run claim.  A \emph{case
0744: witness record} is path-complete when its evidence surface gives a
0745: concrete source or artifact path for each load-bearing component of the row.  A
0746: route-overlap witness has the fields \emph{route identity}, \emph{submitted
0747: sibling}, \emph{overlap reason}, \emph{gate decision}, and \emph{allowed next
0748: actions}.  An artifact-limitation witness has the fields \emph{artifact status
0749: path}, \emph{limitation ledger path}, \emph{observed limitation}, \emph{gate
0750: decision}, and \emph{allowed manuscript claim}.  A promotion witness has the
0751: fields \emph{candidate surface}, \emph{human promotion evidence},
0752: \emph{active-paper markers}, \emph{gate decision}, and \emph{allowed manuscript
0753: claim}.  A theorem-content witness has the fields \emph{changed surface},
0754: \emph{theorem inventory or hard-lint surface}, \emph{substantive-content delta},
0755: \emph{gate decision}, and \emph{allowed manuscript claim}.  The inventory files
0756: \path{ARTIFACT_INVENTORY.md}, \path{SOURCE_MAP.md}, and
0757: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json} are part of the evidence surface
0758: for this paper and give the compact path catalogue used by
0759: Table~\ref{tab:case-witnesses}.
0760: 
0761: We distinguish two evidence levels.  A claim is \emph{path-verified} when this
0762: paper records the relevant source path, artifact path, pinned commit or local
0763: publication path, and gate decision, but does not assert that a command was
0764: freshly rerun for this submission.  A claim is \emph{command-run} when the paper
0765: also records the command, source commit, environment, exit code, and log path
0766: for a fresh run.  This long technical supplement uses path-verified architecture
0767: and case evidence unless explicitly marked command-run.
0768: 
0769: A uniform \emph{pass record} is the finite tuple
0770: \[
0771: \rho=(g,I,d,e,\ell),
0772: \]
0773: where $g$ is the gate name, $I$ is the input surface submitted to that gate,
0774: $d$ is the recorded decision, $e$ is the evidence pointer supporting the
0775: decision, and $\ell$ is the evidence level.  A pass record is assigned to a
0776: claim certificate only when its evidence pointer is contained in the claim's
0777: evidence surface, in a submitted manifest or table row, in a bundled snapshot, or
0778: in an explicit historical-evidence boundary.
0779: 
0780: A \emph{publication certificate} for a load-bearing claim $c$ is the finite
0781: record
0782: \[
0783: \mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B).
0784: \]
0785: Here $K$ is the nonempty finite set of claim kinds carried by $c$, $\ell$ is the
0786: evidence level, $E$ is the evidence surface, $H$ is either a human-promotion
0787: record or the marker that $c$ does not cross the intake-to-publication boundary,
0788: $R$ gives one pass record, with the five fields above, for each gate in
0789: $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, $D$ is present exactly when $K$ contains discovery
0790: and then names the pinned discovery-gate source-interface rows used for the
0791: claim, and $B$ records either the command-run fields or the explicit boundary
0792: that no command-run assertion is made.  A certificate is \emph{locally
0793: checkable} when these finite fields are present and every path, manifest row, or
0794: log named by a field lies in the submitted artifact, the supplemental review
0795: bundle, or the explicit path-verified historical boundary.
0796: 
0797: For later use we make the local check explicit as a finite verifier.  The
0798: concrete package checker supplied with the review bundle is
0799: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}.  Its positive invocation is
0800: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}; it reads exactly
0801: \path{review_bundle/certificate_schema.json},
0802: \path{review_bundle/current_package_pass_records.json},
0803: \path{review_bundle/submission_interface_map.json},
0804: \path{review_bundle/primary_claim_inventory.json}, and
0805: \path{review_bundle/REVIEW_BUNDLE_MANIFEST.json}, and writes the recorded pass log
0806: \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log}.  Its negative-example
0807: invocation is
0808: \path{python review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py --negative-example review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json},
0809: with recorded log \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example_run.md};
0810: that fixture upgrades a route row to command-run strength while omitting the
0811: command-run tuple and is rejected with the finite bad subrecord
0812: \path{neg1: command-run fields incomplete}.  The mathematical verifier below is
0813: the schema abstraction of this package checker.
0814: 
0815: Let \(\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)\) be the seven-kind domain and let
0816: \(\mathsf{Lev}=\{\mathsf{path},\mathsf{command}\}\).  For a candidate record
0817: \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\), define six finite predicates:
0818: \begin{enumerate}
0819: \item[$P_1$:] $K\ne\emptyset$, $K\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$, and
0820: $\ell\in\mathsf{Lev}$.
0821: \item[$P_2$:] $E$ is finite and each item of $E$ is a submitted path,
0822: review-bundle path, manifest row, log path, pinned commit, or explicit
0823: historical-evidence boundary.
0824: \item[$P_3$:] for every gate $g\in\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, $R$ contains a
0825: pass record $(g,I,d,e,\ell')$ whose evidence pointer lies in $E$ or in a named
0826: submitted table, manifest row, bundled snapshot, or historical boundary.
0827: \item[$P_4$:] $H$ records the human promotion exactly when $c$ crosses the
0828: intake-to-publication boundary and otherwise records that no such boundary is
0829: crossed.
0830: \item[$P_5$:] $D$ is present exactly when discovery lies in $K$, and then names
0831: the pinned discovery-gate source-interface rows used by the claim.
0832: \item[$P_6$:] $B$ either records command, source commit, environment, exit code,
0833: and log path for command-run evidence or explicitly records that no command-run
0834: assertion is made.
0835: \end{enumerate}
0836: Define the finite verifier
0837: \[
0838: V(c,\mathcal C)=\bigwedge_{i=1}^{6}P_i(c,\mathcal C,\Gamma).
0839: \]
0840: If this conjunction is false, the rejected subrecord is the finite set of fields
0841: read by the least-indexed failed predicate.  This paragraph defines the verifier
0842: only; no unprinted semantic oracle is part of it.  The next definition packages
0843: the same finite fields as an explicit input type and a finite acceptance table;
0844: the exactness theorem below then proves that the verifier and the independently
0845: named record-acceptance relation coincide on that finite data structure.
0846: 
0847: For a package $P$ with ledger $L$, dispatch table $\Gamma$, and submitted
0848: support surface $S$, write
0849: \[
0850: \mathsf{RGS}_{S}(P,L,\Gamma)
0851: \]
0852: for the following finite predicate.  For every acceptance entry of a
0853: load-bearing claim $c$ in $L$, there is a publication certificate
0854: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ such that $E\subseteq S$, $V(c,\mathcal C)=1$,
0855: each pass record in $R$ is a submitted row of $L$, the row decision is passing,
0856: and the row's evidence pointer denotes an element of $S$ at the evidence level
0857: recorded in the row.  If $B$ does not contain command, source state,
0858: environment, exit code, and log path, then $\mathsf{RGS}_{S}$ asserts only this
0859: finite row-and-pointer property and asserts no implementation-level correctness
0860: of the gate that produced the row.
0861: \end{definition}
0862: 
0863: \begin{definition}[Stronger-reading preorder]
0864: \label{def:stronger-reading-preorder}
0865: Fix the dispatch table $\Gamma$ and certificate tuple
0866: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ of
0867: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.  A proposed reading
0868: $c^{+}$ of the same prose claim is \emph{record-stronger} than $c$, written
0869: $c\preceq_{\Gamma} c^{+}$, if at least one of the following finite coordinates is
0870: strictly enlarged while the others are unchanged or enlarged: the claim-kind set
0871: $K$; the evidence level, from $\mathsf{path}$ to $\mathsf{command}$; the evidence
0872: surface $E$; the promotion field $H$; the assigned gate-pass family $R$; the
0873: discovery-interface field $D$; or the boundary field $B$.  It is
0874: \emph{properly stronger} if the enlargement is strict in at least one coordinate.
0875: It is \emph{accepted as a stronger reading} only when the enlarged tuple still
0876: satisfies $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}$: every newly added kind has its assigned gates,
0877: every newly raised evidence level has the command tuple required by A6, and every
0878: new evidence item is on the submitted support surface or in an explicit
0879: historical boundary.
0880: 
0881: The preorder is intentionally syntactic and finite-record based.  It is not a
0882: semantic entailment relation between mathematical propositions, and it does not
0883: turn an inventory entry, digest row, or path reference into proof evidence for a
0884: theorem-like environment.
0885: \end{definition}
0886: 
0887: \begin{definition}[Certificate-schema acceptance table]
0888: \label{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}
0889: Fix the seven-kind dispatch table $\Gamma$ of
0890: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface}.  The verifier input for a
0891: load-bearing claim $c$ is the following finite record type:
0892: \[
0893: \mathcal C(c)=
0894: \{K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B\}.
0895: \]
0896: Its typed fields are these and only these: $K$ is a finite set of claim kinds;
0897: $\ell\in\{\mathsf{path},\mathsf{command}\}$ is the declared evidence level; $E$
0898: is a finite evidence-surface list; $H$ is the route-boundary field; $R$ is a
0899: finite family of gate pass records, each with gate name, input surface, decision,
0900: evidence pointer, and evidence level; $D$ is either empty or a finite list of
0901: pinned discovery source-interface rows; and $B$ is the command-boundary field.
0902: This is a data schema, not a theorem: all later exactness claims must be proved
0903: from this displayed record and cannot appeal to unlisted package state.  Define
0904: $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathcal C)$ to be the conjunction of the six finite
0905: table predicates A1--A6 displayed below; no clause may query generated
0906: prose, a live daemon, an artifact evaluator, a proof assistant, or a venue state
0907: outside these fields and their cited submitted surfaces.
0908: 
0909: \begin{center}
0910: \centering
0911: \small
0912: \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{p{0.08\linewidth}p{0.22\linewidth}X}
0913: \toprule
0914: Clause & Field read & Acceptance condition \\
0915: \midrule
0916: A1 & $K,\ell$ & $K$ is a nonempty subset of the seven-kind domain and $\ell$ is one of the two evidence levels. \\
0917: A2 & $E$ & Every evidence item is a submitted path, review-bundle path, manifest row, log path, pinned commit, or explicit historical-evidence boundary available at level $\ell$. \\
0918: A3 & $K,R,E$ & For every gate in $\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)$, $R$ has a pass record with gate name, input surface, decision, evidence pointer, and evidence level; the pointer lies in $E$ or in a named submitted table, manifest row, bundled snapshot, or historical boundary. \\
0919: A4 & $H$ & $H$ records human promotion exactly when $c$ crosses the intake-to-publication boundary, and otherwise records that no such crossing occurs. \\
0920: A5 & $K,D$ & $D$ is present exactly when discovery lies in $K$, and then names the pinned discovery-gate source-interface rows used by $c$. \\
0921: A6 & $\ell,B$ & $B$ records command, source commit, environment, exit code, and log path for command-run evidence, or records that no command-run assertion is made. \\
0922: \bottomrule
0923: \end{tabularx}
0924: \end{center}
0925: \end{definition}
0926: 
0927: \begin{proposition}[Finite certificate schema verifier as a record check]
0928: \label{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}
0929: For every load-bearing manuscript claim $c$ whose component kinds lie in the
0930: seven-kind domain, $V(c,\mathcal C)$ is a finite, prose-insensitive
0931: record-schema verifier for the declared publication certificate.  The submitted
0932: package instantiates $V$ by the script
0933: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}.  On the positive package
0934: input it reads the schema file, pass-record ledger, submission-interface map,
0935: primary-claim inventory, and review-bundle manifest named above, and outputs
0936: acceptance in \path{review_bundle/certificate_verification_run.log}.  On the
0937: negative fixture \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json} it
0938: outputs rejection in \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example_run.md}
0939: with the bad subrecord \path{neg1: command-run fields incomplete}.  If $V$
0940: accepts, then $\mathcal C(c)$ is locally checkable at exactly its declared
0941: evidence level.  This is certificate-record verification only: it checks finite
0942: record shape, dispatch, evidence pointers, and command-boundary discipline, not
0943: end-to-end external semantic validation of the referenced artifacts or source
0944: theory.  If $V$ rejects, then a reviewer can extract a finite bad subrecord
0945: consisting of the first failed schema check and the fields on which that check
0946: depends.
0947: \end{proposition}
0948: 
0949: \begin{proof}
0950: The verifier inspects only the displayed tuple $\mathcal C(c)$, the finite
0951: seven-kind domain, the finite dispatch table $\Gamma$, and the finite list of
0952: paths, rows, commits, or logs cited by the record.  The package script realizes
0953: these inputs as five finite JSON files: the schema supplies the seven-kind
0954: domain, evidence levels, gate dispatch table, required record fields, required
0955: pass-record fields, accepted decisions, manifest-required files, and accepted
0956: artifact-prefix patterns; the ledger supplies the seven current records $a_1,
0957: \ldots,a_7$; the submission-interface map and primary-claim inventory supply the
0958: finite manuscript-facing row sets; and the manifest supplies the submitted bundle
0959: paths.  The script output is either the pass transcript in
0960: \path{certificate_verification_run.log} or a list of \path{ERROR: ...} lines,
0961: each naming the failed finite condition.  Since $K$ is required to be a subset of
0962: the seven-kind domain, the set \(\bigcup_{k\in K}\Gamma(k)\) is finite.  The
0963: checks over $E$, $R$, and the command-run fields are therefore finite lookups in
0964: named manuscript or bundle surfaces, not semantic judgments about generated
0965: prose.
0966: 
0967: If all six checks pass, the tuple has exactly the fields required in the
0968: definition of a publication certificate: a finite kind set $K$, declared evidence
0969: level $\ell$, evidence surface $E$, route-boundary field $H$, gate-record field
0970: $R$, optional discovery field $D$ with the required presence condition, and
0971: evidence-strength boundary $B$.  Since $V$ is the conjunction of the explicit
0972: predicates $P_1,\ldots,P_6$, the six table clauses A1--A6 hold for exactly the
0973: same finite inputs.  Check~(ii) is precisely the local-checkability condition on
0974: cited paths, manifest rows, logs, pinned commits, and historical boundaries.
0975: Checks~(iii)--(vi) prevent record-level escalations of the declared level:
0976: missing gate-passage records, unrecorded route promotion, unsupported discovery
0977: routing, and command-run assertion without command-run fields.  Thus an accepted
0978: record is locally checkable at the level $\ell$ it declares, and no stronger
0979: level is silently inferred by the record checker.
0980: 
0981: If a check fails, the verifier stops at a finite test.  The bad subrecord is the
0982: finite collection of fields read by that failed test: for example, $K$ and
0983: $\Gamma$ for a gate-domain failure, $E$ for an evidence-surface failure, $K$,
0984: $\Gamma$, $R$, and the cited evidence item for a gate-record failure, $H$ for a
0985: promotion failure, $K$ and $D$ for a discovery-field failure, or $\ell$ and $B$
0986: for an evidence-level failure.  The supplied negative fixture is exactly the last
0987: case: it declares $\ell=\mathsf{command}$ but provides no
0988: \path{boundary.command_run_fields} containing command, source commit,
0989: environment, exit code, and log path, so the bad subrecord is the pair consisting
0990: of the evidence-level field and the boundary field.  This subrecord is
0991: independent of any surrounding paragraph that may have described the claim.\end{proof}
0992: 
0993: \begin{theorem}[Verifier input--predicate--output contract]
0994: \label{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}
0995: The verifier interface used by the publication-safety theorem is exactly the
0996: following finite mathematical-software contract.  Its input is a pair
0997: $(c,\mathcal C(c))$, where $c$ is a load-bearing manuscript claim and
0998: \(\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\) is the certificate record of
0999: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}, together with the fixed
1000: dispatch table $\Gamma$ and the submitted support surface $S$.  Its checked
1001: predicates and outputs are:
1002: \begin{center}
1003: \centering
1004: \small
1005: \begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{p{0.12\linewidth}p{0.30\linewidth}X}
1006: \toprule
1007: Check & Predicate actually checked & Possible output \\
1008: \midrule
1009: $Q_1$ & $K$ is nonempty, $K\subseteq\mathsf{Dom}(\Gamma)$, and $\ell\in\{\mathsf{path},\mathsf{command}\}$ & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad fields $K,\ell$ \\
1010: $Q_2$ & each item of $E$ is on $S$ or in an explicitly declared pinned, logged, or historical boundary & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad field $E$ and the offending pointer \\
1011: $Q_3$ & every gate assigned by $\Gamma$ to a kind in $K$ has a passing five-field record in $R$ whose pointer is reviewable through $E$, a submitted table, a manifest row, a bundled snapshot, or an historical boundary & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad fields $K,R,E$ and the missing or non-reviewable gate \\
1012: $Q_4$ & $H$ records exactly the human-promotion crossing asserted by $c$, or records that no such crossing is asserted & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad field $H$ \\
1013: $Q_5$ & $D$ is present exactly for discovery-kind claims and then names only pinned source-interface rows used by $c$ & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad fields $K,D$ \\
1014: $Q_6$ & $B$ either contains command, source state, environment, exit code, and log path, or explicitly marks that no command-run assertion is made & accept this coordinate, or reject with bad fields $\ell,B$ \\
1015: \bottomrule
1016: \end{tabularx}
1017: \end{center}
1018: The positive output is only the finite acceptance bit
1019: $\mathsf{accept}(c,\mathcal C)$ for these six predicates.  The negative output is
1020: only the finite bad subrecord belonging to the first failed predicate.  In
1021: particular, the contract has no output channel for proof-assistant validity,
1022: daemon correctness, artifact semantics, fresh case-study reruns, venue upload, or
1023: venue acceptance.  Therefore any theorem in this paper that invokes the verifier
1024: can certify exactly finite record acceptance for the submitted schema and cannot
1025: certify end-to-end workflow correctness unless an enlarged input schema and an
1026: additional proved verifier contract are supplied.
1027: \end{theorem}
1028: 
1029: \begin{proof}
1030: The input type is displayed in
1031: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}: after the fixed table
1032: $\Gamma$ is chosen, the only variable fields are $K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B$.  The
1033: submitted support surface $S$ is used only to decide whether the finite pointers
1034: named in those fields are reviewer-visible at the declared level.  Thus every
1035: listed predicate is a finite Boolean predicate on the displayed input fields and
1036: on the fixed submitted support surface.
1037: 
1038: For each row, the predicate is identical to one of the six predicates used in the
1039: definition of $V$: $Q_i=P_i$ for $1\leq i\leq 6$, with the same fields read and
1040: with the same failure subrecord, namely the fields read by the least-indexed
1041: failed predicate.  Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier}
1042: proves that the bundled Python checker is an implementation witness for this
1043: finite schema: it reads the schema, pass-record ledger, submission-interface map,
1044: primary-claim inventory, and manifest, and returns either the pass transcript or
1045: finite error records.  The theorem here is the abstract verifier contract behind
1046: that implementation witness, so its output alphabet is precisely
1047: \(\{\mathsf{accept}\}\) together with the finite bad-subrecord reports.
1048: 
1049: The final boundary follows by inspection of the input and output alphabets.  No
1050: field in $\mathcal C(c)$ contains an object-language proof derivation, a theorem
1051: prover kernel trace, a daemon transition semantics, an artifact evaluator
1052: semantics, a fresh rerun transcript for the historical case studies, or a venue
1053: receipt.  No output of the contract returns such an object either.  A conclusion
1054: depending on one of those objects is therefore not a function of the declared
1055: input record.  It can become checkable only after the schema is enlarged to add
1056: that object as an input coordinate and after a new verifier contract for the
1057: enlarged coordinate is proved.  Hence the present verifier certifies only finite
1058: record acceptance and its finite rejection witnesses.\end{proof}
1059: 
1060: \begin{theorem}[Schema-relative finite-record acceptance exactness]
1061: \label{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}
1062: Fix the seven-kind dispatch table $\Gamma$, the two evidence levels, and the
1063: typed certificate tuple
1064: $\mathcal C(c)=(K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)$ of
1065: Definition~\ref{def:certificate-schema-acceptance-table}.  For every
1066: load-bearing claim $c$ and every finite candidate certificate $\mathcal C(c)$,
1067: the finite verifier $V$ of
1068: Definition~\ref{def:operational-acceptance-interface} and
1069: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract} accepts $\mathcal C(c)$ if
1070: and only if the six-clause table predicate
1071: $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathcal C)$ holds.  This is a schema-relative and
1072: finite-record-relative assertion: both sides read exactly the fields named in the
1073: fixed schema and in the finite submitted record surface, and neither side ranges
1074: over unrecorded semantic facts about the referenced mathematics, artifacts, or
1075: gate implementations.  Moreover, if $V$ accepts a record for $c$, the same record
1076: cannot certify any stronger reading $c^{+}$ that adds a new claim kind, a stronger
1077: evidence level, an omitted route crossing, an omitted discovery interface, an
1078: omitted pass record, or a command-run assertion: such a reading has a finite bad
1079: subrecord of the corresponding clause.  Thus $V$ is exact for this paper's finite
1080: record-acceptance relation over the submitted source, tables, manifests, logs,
1081: and explicitly declared historical boundaries, while remaining silent about
1082: external semantic validity of the referenced theory or artifacts and about the
1083: implementation-level soundness of any deployed gate implementation beyond the
1084: recorded pass rows.
1085: \end{theorem}
1086: 
1087: \begin{proof}
1088: The definition of $V$ and the six clauses above use the same finite inputs.
1089: Predicate $P_1$ is clause~A1, $P_2$ is clause~A2, and $P_3$ is clause~A3,
1090: including both assigned-gate coverage and the requirement that each evidence
1091: pointer be tied to the declared review surface.  Predicates $P_4$, $P_5$, and
1092: $P_6$ are exactly clauses~A4, A5, and A6.  Therefore, if
1093: $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathcal C)$ holds, every verifier predicate is true
1094: and $V$ accepts.
1095: 
1096: Conversely, suppose $V$ accepts.  Reading the successful checks in order gives
1097: precisely clauses~1--6: the kind set and evidence level are in the permitted
1098: domains; the evidence surface is reviewer-available at the declared level; every
1099: gate assigned by $\Gamma$ has a pass record with the required five fields and an
1100: allowed pointer; promotion and non-promotion are separated by $H$; discovery-kind
1101: claims and only discovery-kind claims carry $D$; and $B$ fixes the command-run
1102: boundary.  Hence $\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}(c,\mathcal C)$ holds.
1103: 
1104: For the final assertion, keep the accepted tuple fixed and consider a proposed
1105: stronger reading $c^{+}$.  If $c^{+}$ adds a claim kind, the finite set of gates
1106: assigned by $\Gamma$ grows unless the added kind is vacuous in this interface;
1107: without the corresponding pass records clause~3 fails, and if the kind lies
1108: outside the seven-kind domain clause~1 fails.  If $c^{+}$ raises the evidence
1109: level to command-run, clause~6 fails unless the command, commit, environment,
1110: exit code, and log path are present.  If it adds a route crossing, clause~4 fails
1111: unless $H$ contains the human-promotion record.  If it adds discovery content,
1112: clause~5 fails unless the pinned discovery rows are named.  If it asserts a new
1113: gate-dependent conclusion without a pass record, clause~3 fails.  In each case
1114: the failed clause reads only finitely many fields, so deleting irrelevant unread
1115: fields gives an inclusion-minimal bad subrecord.  If none of the clauses changes,
1116: then $c^{+}$ is not stronger in the record semantics: the certificate still
1117: licenses only the same kind set, same evidence level, same gate records, same
1118: route boundary, same discovery interface, and same command boundary.\end{proof}
1119: 
1120: \begin{corollary}[Executable schema exactness and negative fixture]
1121: \label{cor:executable-schema-exactness-negative-fixture}
1122: Under the hypotheses and fixed schema of
1123: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, the executable verifier instance
1124: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py} is a finite witness of the
1125: same schema-relative relation, not an additional semantic oracle.  On the positive
1126: submitted package it can witness exactly the records satisfying
1127: \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}\) at their declared evidence levels.  On the negative
1128: fixture
1129: \path{review_bundle/negative_certificate_record_example.json}, which upgrades a
1130: route record to command-run strength while omitting the command, source state,
1131: environment, exit code, and log path, the extracted bad subrecord is exactly the
1132: evidence-level declaration together with the incomplete boundary field.  Hence
1133: the executable example separates the verifier contract from any stronger reading:
1134: changing prose or ambient pipeline state while keeping the finite schema fields
1135: fixed cannot change either the acceptance bit or the first failed finite
1136: subrecord.
1137: \end{corollary}
1138: 
1139: \begin{proof}
1140: Proposition~\ref{prop:finite-certificate-schema-verifier} identifies the script
1141: \path{review_bundle/verify_certificate_records.py}, the schema files it reads,
1142: and the two recorded runs.  Its positive run returns the finite acceptance
1143: transcript for the submitted records, and its negative run returns the reported
1144: finite error \path{neg1: command-run fields incomplete}.  By
1145: Theorem~\ref{thm:verifier-input-output-contract}, those are the only two output
1146: kinds of the verifier contract: acceptance of the six predicates or a bad
1147: subrecord for the first failed predicate.  By
1148: Theorem~\ref{thm:record-acceptance-exactness}, positive acceptance is equivalent
1149: to \(\mathsf{Acc}_{\Gamma}\) for the same tuple
1150: \((K,\ell,E,H,R,D,B)\).  In the negative fixture, clauses A1--A4 and the
1151: non-discovery discipline are supplied for the route row, while the declared
1152: command-run level triggers clause A6 and the required five command-run fields are
1153: absent.  The first failed predicate therefore reads exactly \(\ell\) and \(B\),
1154: so deleting any unrelated prose, score, route history, or ambient implementation
1155: state leaves the same failure.  The script is thus an executable finite-record
1156: witness for the schema-relative theorem and for its negative certificate example,
1157: not evidence of an unrecorded semantic rerun or gate-implementation proof.\end{proof}
